  <h3>Missing or partial plans</h3>

  <p>For many older buildings, the original plans are lost, or only a few pages survive. Sometimes the only plan on file is a small sketch from a real estate listing, which is not accurate enough to build from.</p>

  <h2>Side by side</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>Original plans</th><th>As-built drawings</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>What they show</strong></td><td>What was designed</td><td>What is there today</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>When they are made</strong></td><td>Before construction</td><td>Any time after, by measuring the building</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Based on</strong></td><td>The designer's intent</td><td>Real measurements</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Include later changes</strong></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Safe to design and order from</strong></td><td>Only after checking</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Which one do you need?</h2>

  <p>If you are changing the building in any way, you need to know what is there now. That means as-builts.</p>

  <p>Original plans are still worth keeping. They often show things you cannot see, such as how the structure was framed or where pipes were meant to run. The best case is to have both: the old plans for background, and current as-builts for real sizes.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">A quick test for your old plans</div>
    <p>Pick three things you can check with a tape measure: the length of one room, the width of one door, and the distance from a window to a corner. If any of them is off by more than an inch from the plans, do not design or order materials from those plans.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>One more term: "record drawings"</h2>

  <p>On large construction projects, the contractor marks up the plans during the job to show what changed. Those are sometimes called as-builts or record drawings. They are only as good as the notes that were kept, and they stop being correct the first time the building is changed afterward.</p>

  <p>When we say as-built drawings, we mean drawings made by measuring the building as it stands today.</p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>

  <p>Original plans tell you what was supposed to be built. As-built drawings tell you what is really there. For any project that changes a building, start with what is really there. New to all of this? Read <a href="/resources/what-are-as-built-drawings/">What Are As-Built Drawings?</a> or <a href="/quote/">get an instant quote</a>.</p>
